| short     | Short     | 2             |
| int       | Integer   | 4             |
| long      | Long      | 8             |
| float     | Float     | 4             |
| double    | Double    | 8             |
| char      | Character | 2             |
| boolean   | Boolean   | JVM dependent |




Types of Variables

Java has three major types.

1. Local Variable

        Declared inside a method.

        Example

            public static void main(String[] args){

                int age = 20;

            }

        Properties

            Created when method starts
            Destroyed when method ends
            No default value
            Accessible only inside the method

2. Instance Variable

        Declared inside class.

            Outside methods.

            class Student{

                int age;

            }

        Properties

            Belongs to an object
            Every object gets its own copy

3. Static Variable

        Declared using

            static

            Example

            class Student{

                static String college = "ADYPU";

            }

        Properties

            Shared by all objects
            Only one copy exists
            Variable Scope

Scope ?

Where can we use the variable?

Example

public class Demo{

    public static void main(String[] args){

        int age = 20;

        System.out.println(age);

    }

}


    Variable	Lifetime

    Local	    Until method ends
    Instance	Until object is destroyed
    Static	    Until program ends


Default Values
    Only Instance variables, Static variables, receive default values.

    Type	    Default
    int	        0
    double	    0.0
    boolean	    false
    char	    '\u0000'
    String	    null

Local variables must be initialized before use.